WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.649
Rosa receives a mailer offering a disability income policy for thirty dollars

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:11.297
less a month than the one Theo sold her two years ago.

00:00:11.297 --> 00:00:16.475
Replacement means dropping or reducing existing coverage to buy new coverage.

00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:21.182
It is lawful and sometimes wise, but it resets clocks.

00:00:21.182 --> 00:00:26.360
A new disability or long-term care policy brings a new pre-existing

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.538
condition look-back, a new probationary period and a new contestable period.

00:00:31.538 --> 00:00:35.775
Rosa’s back condition, now covered after two years, could

00:00:35.775 --> 00:00:39.541
become a pre-existing condition under the new policy.

00:00:39.541 --> 00:00:45.660
The first replacement question is always: what coverage has the client already earned?

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.484
Price is one line in a comparison.

00:00:48.484 --> 00:00:51.712
Theo lays the two policies side by side.

00:00:51.712 --> 00:00:56.554
Does the new policy define disability as own occupation or any occupation?

00:00:56.554 --> 00:01:00.590
How long is the elimination period and the benefit period?

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:05.835
Does it include a residual benefit, a cost-of-living rider, a future increase option?

00:01:05.835 --> 00:01:09.063
Is it noncancelable, guaranteed renewable or conditionally renewable?

00:01:09.063 --> 00:01:12.694
What does it exclude that the old policy covers?

00:01:12.694 --> 00:01:15.922
For medical plans, is the current plan grandfathered

00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:18.747
with features a new plan would lose?

00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:22.378
Only after the benefits, limitations and exclusions are compared

00:01:22.378 --> 00:01:26.010
can anyone say whether thirty dollars is a saving.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.063
The new insurer will underwrite Rosa as she is

00:01:29.063 --> 00:01:31.778
today, not as she was two years ago.

00:01:31.778 --> 00:01:36.527
Her back condition could produce a higher rate, an exclusion rider or a declination.

00:01:36.527 --> 00:01:41.277
The cardinal rule of replacement is that the existing policy is never canceled until

00:01:41.277 --> 00:01:45.688
the new policy is issued, delivered and accepted on terms the client understands.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:48.402
A client who cancels first and is then

00:01:48.402 --> 00:01:51.116
declined has lost coverage she cannot get back.

00:01:51.116 --> 00:01:54.848
Theo also checks whether the old policy’s premium is locked by

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:58.580
a noncancelable clause, a guarantee the new policy may not match.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.534
Two products carry specific Maryland replacement rules.

00:02:01.534 --> 00:02:06.598
When a Medicare supplement policy replaces another, the new insurer must credit

00:02:06.598 --> 00:02:11.662
the time already served and may not impose new pre-existing condition, waiting,

00:02:11.662 --> 00:02:15.882
elimination or probationary periods for benefits the old policy covered.

00:02:15.882 --> 00:02:19.258
Long-term care replacement works the same way, with

00:02:19.258 --> 00:02:22.634
a replacement notice and a comparison of benefits.

00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:28.542
Both require the producer to ask about existing coverage and to document the answer.

00:02:28.542 --> 00:02:34.028
Selling Gloria a second Medigap policy without replacing the first is prohibited duplication.

00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:38.670
The producer’s job is to make replacement a deliberate, documented decision.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.544
Replacement is right when the new policy fixes a real gap:

00:02:42.544 --> 00:02:46.419
a short benefit period, an any-occupation definition, a conditionally renewable clause,

00:02:46.419 --> 00:02:50.293
or a premium the client can no longer afford at all.

00:02:50.293 --> 00:02:54.872
It is right when the client’s health still qualifies for standard rates and

00:02:54.872 --> 00:02:59.099
the new policy’s waiting periods will not leave a known condition uncovered.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.678
It is wrong when the only gain is a small premium saving that

00:03:03.678 --> 00:03:07.904
the client pays for with lost guarantees or a newly excluded condition.

00:03:07.904 --> 00:03:10.722
Theo writes down the reasons either way; a

00:03:10.722 --> 00:03:13.540
documented recommendation protects the client and the producer.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.189
Before recommending any health insurance replacement, work the list.

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:22.321
One: identify the coverage the client has already

00:03:22.321 --> 00:03:25.937
earned, including satisfied pre-existing and probationary periods.

00:03:25.937 --> 00:03:31.619
Two: compare definitions, periods, riders, renewability and exclusions, not just premium.

00:03:31.619 --> 00:03:36.268
Three: confirm the client’s current insurability and get the

00:03:36.268 --> 00:03:40.917
new policy issued and accepted before anything is canceled.

00:03:40.917 --> 00:03:46.600
Four: apply Medigap and long-term care replacement rules where they exist.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.732
Five: document the comparison and the client’s decision.

00:03:50.732 --> 00:03:55.381
A replacement that survives the checklist serves the client;

00:03:55.381 --> 00:04:00.030
one that does not is a sale, not advice.
